WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.850
"Spider-Man: No Way Home" is one of the most
thrilling movies in the Marvel Cinematic Universe

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:08.010
– but does any of it actually make sense?

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:12.840
When it comes to time travel, parallel universes,
and magic, anything is possible — and when

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:18.699
anything is possible, the rules for how things
work can get real confusing, real fast.

00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:23.429
In order to reverse the effects of the movie's
first botched spell, Doctor Strange casts

00:00:23.429 --> 00:00:27.789
another spell at the climax of the movie that
makes everyone in the multiverse forget that

00:00:27.789 --> 00:00:30.580
the MCU's Peter Parker ever existed.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:34.180
This complicated series of events can be tough
to wrap your head around.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.180
In the end, Doctor Strange's spell in "No
Way Home" only raised more questions, even

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:42.410
as it resolved the multiverse-level catastrophe
at hand.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:46.360
One of the most glaring questions raised by
the effects of Doctor Strange's spell is in

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.360
how it influences Happy Hogan.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.050
A key ongoing subplot running throughout the
first two Tom Holland "Spider-Man" films is

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:56.440
Happy Hogan's budding relationship with Aunt
May.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.570
The long-time accomplice to Tony Stark meets
May through Peter, and their relationship

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:01.980
grows from there.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:06.240
Given Happy's ties to both Peter and May,
at first glance, it doesn't make much sense

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.039
that he would still remember her after Doctor
Strange casts his second spell.

00:01:11.039 --> 00:01:15.520
Peter runs into Happy while visiting May's
grave after she is killed by the Green Goblin.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:20.799
He doesn't recognize Peter, but he does briefly
explain that he met May "through Spider-Man."

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:25.380
Since Spider-Man still exists as a separate
entity from Peter in everyone's minds, Happy

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:30.039
would still have memories of working with
a young superhero on Avengers missions.

00:01:30.039 --> 00:01:35.359
However, any inkling that that young superhero
is the man he's talking to at the graveside

00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:36.689
is gone.

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:41.630
This fact is also hammered home in J. Jonah
Jameson's final rant, in which he explicitly

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.929
asks for the now-unknown Spider-Man to reveal
his true identity.

00:01:45.929 --> 00:01:50.459
And so, even though the specialness of Happy's
bond with Peter through their connection to

00:01:50.459 --> 00:01:55.090
May is gone, there is something nice about
him retaining memories of the woman he loved

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:58.979
— even if the details about who introduced
them are a bit fuzzier.

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:02.759
Some of the dialogue between Peter and Michelle
Jones-Watson near the end of "Spider-Man:

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:07.399
No Way Home" indicates that it is possible
for MJ, and presumably others, to remember

00:02:07.399 --> 00:02:09.750
Peter even after the spell.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:14.620
They only need to be reminded, something which
Peter is too chicken to do in the film's conclusion.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.040
If that's the case, then wouldn't Mysterio's
video, which is broadcast at the end of "Spider-Man:

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.900
Far From Home," simply remind everyone that
Peter Parker both exists and that he is Spider-Man?

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.239
This is a question "No Way Home" never really
addresses; it is left up to the viewer's imagination

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:30.510
to fill in the gaps.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:34.930
Presumably, Doctor Strange's spell could have
done a number of things to resolve this, including

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:40.150
altering reality to change the video or blocking
the true information from reaching people's

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:41.939
minds when they watch it.

00:02:41.939 --> 00:02:46.720
In either case, it is assumed that the video
is incapable of reminding the world of Peter

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:47.720
Parker.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:50.980
Even so, there's probably a good amount of
people who still believe that Spider-Man killed

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:56.980
Mysterio, attracted the alternate-universe
villains, and is actually a web-slinging menace.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.370
The second spell also raises another question.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:03.170
If his second spell — which makes everyone
in the universe forget Peter Parker's existence

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:07.440
— is capable of banishing the invaders coming
through from the multiverse, does it also

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.200
make the entire multiverse forget about every
Peter Parker?

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.349
This is another matter which "No Way Home"
doesn't directly address.

00:03:14.349 --> 00:03:18.650
Like Mysterio's tape, this explanation requires
a bit of imagination since we don't know the

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:22.239
specifics of the ex-Sorcerer Supreme's spellcasting.

00:03:22.239 --> 00:03:26.599
It's entirely possible that while the first
spell applied to every Peter Parker in every

00:03:26.599 --> 00:03:30.060
timeline, the second only applies to the MCU's
Peter.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:35.059
This way, the Raimi-verse and Webb-verse Peters
– AKA Tobey Maguire and Andrew Garfield

00:03:35.059 --> 00:03:40.390
– would still be remembered in their universes
while also being sent home safe and sound.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:44.640
They might, however, forget about their adventure
with MCU Peter.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.469
Only time will tell how this multiverse of
madness is going to shake out.

